{"units": [{"id": "pistos-word2", "translation": "There was no remedy, neither edible, nor anointable, nor"}, {"id": "pistos-word3", "translation": "to be born as a son to father Zeus"}, {"id": "pistos-word4", "translation": "it nurtured shield-bearing inhabitants"}, {"id": "pistos-word5", "translation": "so that you may be faithful to this duty"}, {"id": "pistos-word6", "translation": "faithful"}, {"id": "pistos-word7", "translation": "a woman"}, {"id": "pistos-word8", "translation": "faithful"}, {"id": "pistos-word9", "translation": "and faithful"}, {"id": "pistos-word10", "translation": "faithful"}, {"id": "pistos-word11", "translation": "so that"}, {"id": "pistos-word12", "translation": "and you have come with one who is wise"}, {"id": "pistos-word13", "translation": "the pledges"}, {"id": "pistos-word14", "translation": "These things of the Persians who have departed"}, {"id": "pistos-word15", "translation": "but you must"}, {"id": "pistos-word16", "translation": "O faithful"}, {"id": "pistos-word17", "translation": "your"}, {"id": "pistos-word18", "translation": "Xenophon"}, {"id": "pistos-word19", "translation": "Cyrus with those present of the"}, {"id": "pistos-word20", "translation": "For some"}, {"id": "pistos-word21", "translation": "having heard"}, {"id": "pistos-word22", "translation": "to us indeed"}, {"id": "pistos-word23", "translation": "Where then are the remaining oracles of Loxias, the Pythian-uttered?"}, {"id": "pistos-word24", "translation": "faithful"}, {"id": "pistos-word25", "translation": "unless something"}, {"id": "pistos-word26", "translation": "For the mortal race thinks day by day, and nothing is more trustworthy than"}, {"id": "pistos-word27", "translation": "the pledges"}, {"id": "pistos-word28", "translation": "For fire and sea and the"}, {"id": "pistos-word29", "translation": "And may these things remain forever, to love the pledges of these"}, {"id": "pistos-word30", "translation": "faithful"}, {"id": "pistos-word31", "translation": "faithful"}, {"id": "pistos-word32", "translation": "with the high-flying thunderclaps"}, {"id": "pistos-word33", "translation": "with bow-drawing spirit"}, {"id": "pistoma-orth1", "translation": "pledge, assurance"}, {"id": "pistoma-def1", "translation": "that by which faith is given to something, pledge, confirmation."}, {"id": "pistoma-note1", "translation": "of Hera Teleia and Zeus"}, {"id": "pistoma-note2", "translation": "and Oath"}, {"id": "pistoma-note3", "translation": "transferred to people in whom one has faith"}, {"id": "pistoma-note4", "translation": "Persians, aged"}, {"id": "pistoma-note5", "translation": "who are called the faithful in v. 2, about which the scholiast talks nonsense. Pistheira by syncope. It is a Persian city."}, {"id": "pistoma-quote1", "translation": "pledges"}, {"id": "pistoma-quote2", "translation": "he abides by his pledges"}, {"id": "pistoma-quote3", "translation": "pledges"}, {"id": "pistoma-word1", "translation": "of Hera Teleia and Zeus"}, {"id": "pistoma-word2", "translation": "and Oath"}, {"id": "pistoma-word3", "translation": "Persians, aged"}, {"id": "pistoma-word4", "translation": "the pledges"}, {"id": "pistoma-word5", "translation": "Pistheira by syncope. It is a Persian city."}, {"id": "pisyos-orth1", "translation": "trusting, relying on"}, {"id": "pisyos-def1", "translation": "trusting, relying on."}, {"id": "pisyos-note1", "translation": "in the gods"}, {"id": "pisyos-note2", "translation": "trusting in the finely built"}, {"id": "pisyos-note3", "translation": "in strength"}, {"id": "pisyos-quote1", "translation": "trusting"}, {"id": "pisyos-quote2", "translation": "cables"}, {"id": "pisyos-quote3", "translation": "trusting"}, {"id": "pisyos-word1", "translation": "in the gods"}, {"id": "pisyos-word2", "translation": "trusting in the finely built"}, {"id": "pisyos-word3", "translation": "in strength"}, {"id": "pitno-orth1", "translation": "I fall"}, {"id": "pitno-def1", "translation": "I fall."}, {"id": "pitno-def2", "translation": "with eis"}, {"id": "pitno-def3", "translation": "with en"}, {"id": "pitno-note1", "translation": "It is a poetic form of the verb pipto (like mimno for meno), which the tragedians often used. Moreover, in the manuscripts of the tragedians there is a frequent accentual error pitnein and pitnon, and likewise in the compounds empitno and prospitno. The correct accent has generally been preserved by the older manuscripts, among them the Medicean of Sophocles, and the ancient grammarians attest it: on which I have spoken in Thes. vol. 6 p. 1128 after Elmsley on Eur. Heracl. 77 and Med. 53."}, {"id": "pitno-note2", "translation": "the one"}, {"id": "pitno-note3", "translation": "of one falling"}, {"id": "pitno-note4", "translation": "when"}, {"id": "pitno-note5", "translation": "pitnei M"}, {"id": "pitno-note6", "translation": "disturbance into the mind"}, {"id": "pitno-note7", "translation": "here too pitnei M"}, {"id": "pitno-note8", "translation": "Fear – heavy in fear upon women in the house"}, {"id": "pitno-note9", "translation": "pitnon M"}, {"id": "pitno-note10", "translation": "and it falls into the watery vessel"}, {"id": "pitno-note11", "translation": "where pitnei was written by the first hand of M, changed to pipei (i.e. piptei) by the second."}, {"id": "pitno-quote1", "translation": "one falls, and another lifts the three-pronged"}, {"id": "pitno-quote2", "translation": "the house of Justice falls"}, {"id": "pitno-quote3", "translation": "falls"}, {"id": "pitno-quote4", "translation": "falling"}, {"id": "pitno-word1", "translation": "I fall"}, {"id": "pitno-word2", "translation": "I remain"}, {"id": "pitno-word3", "translation": "I remain"}, {"id": "pitno-word4", "translation": "to fall"}, {"id": "pitno-word5", "translation": "falling"}, {"id": "pitno-word6", "translation": "I fall upon"}, {"id": "pitno-word7", "translation": "I fall before"}, {"id": "pitno-word8", "translation": "the one"}, {"id": "pitno-word9", "translation": "of one falling"}, {"id": "pitno-word10", "translation": "when"}, {"id": "pitno-word11", "translation": "falls"}, {"id": "pitno-word12", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "pitno-word13", "translation": "disturbance into the mind"}, {"id": "pitno-word14", "translation": "falls M"}, {"id": "pitno-word15", "translation": "in"}, {"id": "pitno-word16", "translation": "Fear – heavy in fear upon women in the house"}, {"id": "pitno-word17", "translation": "falling M"}, {"id": "pitno-word18", "translation": "and it falls into the watery vessel"}, {"id": "pitno-word19", "translation": "falls"}, {"id": "pitno-word20", "translation": "falls"}, {"id": "pitno-word21", "translation": "falls"}, {"id": "pitylos-orth1", "translation": "beat of oars, rhythm"}, {"id": "pitylos-def1", "translation": "which properly signifies the sound of water struck by an oar; the tragedians used it of the oars themselves and of rowing (see Thes. vol. 6 p. 1131) and transferred it to other things."}, {"id": "pitylos-note1", "translation": "It is said of the noise of clapping hands"}, {"id": "pitylos-note2", "translation": "row with your hands a processional beat around the head"}, {"id": "pitylos-note3", "translation": "as Hesychius explains: a twisting of the hand, when it is brought down rapidly, and Pollux 2, 147: the continuous gathering of the hands brought down rapidly in a crowd."}, {"id": "pitylos-note4", "translation": "all with one"}, {"id": "pitylos-note5", "translation": "where the Byzantine scholiast explains: by the din of battle and the shouting."}, {"id": "pitylos-quote1", "translation": "a beat"}, {"id": "pitylos-quote2", "translation": "with a beat — wretched ones, they gasp on the dry land"}]}